= Set 1: Elementary Group Theory

#problem("Cube power map")[
  Suppose $(x y)^3 = x^3 y^3$ for all $x, y in G$, and $G$ has no elements of order $3$. Prove $G$ is abelian.

  #meta[★★][power maps / forcing commutativity][no close analogue; same flavor as Spring 2007 #3, Fall 2017 #2]
]
#proof[
  We will prove this result as a series of claims. The first claim is that $(y x)^2 = x^2 y^2$. $        (x y)^3 & = x^3 y^3 \
  => x y x y x y & = x^3 y^3 \
      => (y x)^2 & = x^2 y^2. $ Consider $p: G -> G$ where $x -> x^3$, the assumption about the group tells us that $p$ is a homomorphism furthermore the fact that no element has order 3, $p$ is injective. Suppose $ x^3 y^3 = y^3 x^3 => p(x y) = p(y x) => x y = y x. $ Thus if the cubes of two elements commute then the two elements themselves commute. Now we will prove that $ x^3 y^2 = y^2 x^3 $ for any pair $x$ and $y$. $ x^3 y^2 & = x x^2 y^2 \
          & = x y x y x \
          & = (x y)^2 x \
          & = y^2 x^2 x = y^2 x^3. $

  Now we will prove that everything commutes with squares. $ x^3 (y^2)^3 & = (y^2)^3 x^3 quad (x^3 y^2 = y^2 x^3 "repeatedly"). $

  Thus $x y^2 = y^2 x$. $ y x y x & = (y x)^2 \
          & = x^2 y^2 \
          & = y^2 x^2 \
      x y & = y x. $


]

#problem("Central quotient and the 5/8 theorem")[
  Prove that if $G\/Z(G)$ is cyclic, then $G$ is abelian. Then prove the "5/8 theorem": if $G$ is finite and nonabelian, the probability that two random elements commute is at most $5/8$.

  #meta[★ (first claim), ★★ (5/8)][centers, class equation / counting commuting pairs][Fall 2001 G1, Fall 2004 Problem 1, Spring 2001 A2, Fall 2011 Problem 2]
]
#proof[
  Suppose $lcoset(G, Z(G))$ is cyclic. Thus exists $g in G$ such that $lcoset(G, Z(G)) = chevron.l g Z(G) chevron.r$. Thus for all $a, b in G$, $a = g^k z_1$ and $b = g^m z_2$. Hence $ a b & = g^k z_1 g^m z_2 \
      & = g^m z_2 g^k z_1 \
      & = b a. $ Thus $G$ is abelian.
]

#problem("Unions of subgroups")[
  Prove that no group is a union of two proper subgroups. Find a group that is a union of three. For a challenge, characterize all such groups.

  #meta[★★ (challenge ★★★)][covering arguments, counting via quotients][Spring 2016 #9, Spring 2004 Problem 1]
]
#proof[
  Assume for the sake of contradiction, $G = H union K$ where $H, K$ are proper. Suppose $h in H - K$ and $k in K - H$. $h k in G$. We have various cases:
  1. $h k in H$: Then $h^(-1) h k = k in H$ which is a contradiction.
  2. $h k in K$: Then $h = h k k^(-1) in K$, which is a contradiction.
  Thus $h k in.not H union K = G$, thus a contradiction occurs.
]

